  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Three pigeons once sat in the nose of a missile. Not as cargo. As the guidance system, pecking at a screen.",
        "visual_query": "homing pigeon close flying",
        "visual_fallback": "pigeon flying"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Lenses threw the target onto that screen. The bird pecks where the ship is, the screen tilts, sensors read the angle, the missile turns.",
        "visual_query": "1940s laboratory animal experiment",
        "visual_fallback": "lab bench"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And it worked. One pigeon pecked over ten thousand times in forty-five minutes without stopping. Birds don't panic and don't get distracted.",
        "visual_query": "vintage missile nose cone",
        "visual_fallback": "missile nose"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The defence committee called it eccentric and impractical. Then handed over 25,000 dollars anyway, and cancelled the whole thing in October 1944.",
        "visual_query": "1940s military office archival",
        "visual_fallback": "military office"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Navy quietly restarted it in 1948 as Project Orcon, and finally killed it in 1953, when electronics got reliable enough.",
        "visual_query": "1950s navy ship deck",
        "visual_fallback": "navy ship"
      }
    ],
